{"reference":"Hebrews 4","book":"Hebrews","chapter":4,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The argument about rest","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The discussion of “rest” in Hebrews 3–4 deliberately brings together the wilderness generation, the land, Sabbath, creation, present access, and a future promise. The term is therefore not reduced to a single destination or experience; the chapter’s repeated call to enter God’s rest holds these strands together.","verse_refs":["Hebrews 10:1-39","Hebrews 11:1-40","Hebrews 12:1-29","Hebrews 13:1-25","Hebrews 1:1-14","Hebrews 2:1-18","Hebrews 3:1-19","Hebrews 3:7-19","Hebrews 5:1-14","Hebrews 6:1-20","Hebrews 7:1-28","Hebrews 8:1-13","Hebrews 9:1-28"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_hebrews_004_c95c07dfedb4470489ecd3f6"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"language_literary","title":"The searching word","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"In verses 12–13, “the word of God” is portrayed as living, active, penetrating, and able to discern inner thoughts and intentions. The image moves immediately to complete exposure before God, so it functions within the chapter’s warning about hearing God’s speech and being accountable to him. Some readers identify this “word” directly with Jesus, but that identification is not explicit in the grammar of this passage.","verse_refs":["Hebrews 4:12-13"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_hebrews_004_434dbab92014326b2ea7d656","reader_path_hebrews_004_9feb63aa4945b7f92bc1fd6e"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Joshua and the remaining rest","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Verse 8 argues that Joshua did not provide the final rest envisioned here, because Scripture still speaks later of another day. Christian connections sometimes made between Rahab, rest, and later biblical writings should be kept distinct from the land and covenant claims of the book of Joshua itself.","verse_refs":["Hebrews 4:8-11"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_hebrews_004_918d1bc804d0f3e9a84db5c1"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Approaching with confidence","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"The chapter closes by grounding perseverance in Jesus as the great high priest. His priestly role supports both holding firmly to the confession and approaching the throne of grace with confidence: the expected result is mercy and help in time of need.","verse_refs":["Hebrews 4:14-16"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_hebrews_004_1b2ae9172664a45a72dea5a8"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}